From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Albin Bernhardsson <albin.bernhardsson@arm.com>
Date: Mon, 22 Jun 2026 09:55:52 -0700
Subject: Vulkan: Fix device loss handling

Always wait for the device work to finish and destroy its objects before
destroying the device, even when the device is lost. This is required in
Vulkan according to spec.

Bug: 500417361
Change-Id: I89cedcd15b660a1a4e9a62099fc22e56c1c37a37
Reviewed-on: https://dawn-review.googlesource.com/c/dawn/+/314796
Reviewed-by: Corentin Wallez <cwallez@chromium.org>
Reviewed-by: David Neto <dneto@google.com>
Commit-Queue: David Neto <dneto@google.com>

Electron adaptation notes (42-x-y):
- This branch predates the upstream device-cleanup refactor
  (dawn 302578 / 309569): DeviceBase::Destroy() still treats
  State::BeingDisconnected as unreachable and HandleError() performs
  the wait-for-idle teardown inline for unexpected errors. The
  upstream hunk (move a lost device to BeingDisconnected and let
  Destroy() wait) therefore has no deferred path to attach to.
- Ported inline instead: on a real (non-injected) DeviceLost,
  HandleError() now transitions Alive -> BeingDisconnected and runs
  the same WaitForIdleForDestruction()/TickImpl()/
  AssumeCommandsComplete() sequence the branch already uses for
  unexpected errors before settling on State::Disconnected, instead
  of jumping straight to Disconnected without waiting. The
  ErrorInjector (fuzzer-only) path and the unexpected-error path are
  unchanged.
- Toggles.h/Toggles.cpp: context drift only (the branch carries a
  later-merged toggle at the insertion point); QueueVk.cpp include
  spelled "dawn/utils/SystemUtils.h" to match this branch's include
  roots. Device.h, PhysicalDeviceVk.cpp and the QueueVk.cpp code
  hunk applied as upstream.

diff --git a/src/dawn/native/Device.cpp b/src/dawn/native/Device.cpp
index e9be296cebf058df921f0daa942acac7d3fbc94f..1a09baca9fa6c1e91aa417c3681964f76a76611c 100644
--- a/src/dawn/native/Device.cpp
+++ b/src/dawn/native/Device.cpp
@@ -696,19 +696,31 @@ void DeviceBase::HandleError(std::unique_ptr<ErrorData> error,
         InternalErrorType::Validation | InternalErrorType::DeviceLost | additionalAllowedErrors;
 
     if (type == InternalErrorType::DeviceLost) {
-        mState = State::Disconnected;
-
-        // If the ErrorInjector is enabled, then the device loss might be fake and the device
-        // still be executing commands. Force a wait for idle in this case, with State being
-        // Disconnected so we can detect this case in WaitForIdleForDestruction.
         if (ErrorInjectorEnabled()) {
+            // If the ErrorInjector is enabled, then the device loss might be fake and the device
+            // still be executing commands. Force a wait for idle in this case, with State being
+            // Disconnected so we can detect this case in WaitForIdleForDestruction.
+            mState = State::Disconnected;
             IgnoreErrors(mQueue->WaitForIdleForDestruction());
             IgnoreErrors(TickImpl());
+            mQueue->AssumeCommandsComplete();
+        } else {
+            // A real device lost happened. Always wait for the device work to finish and destroy
+            // its objects before disconnecting, even when the device is lost. This is required in
+            // Vulkan according to spec. Transition to a non-alive state if we are currently alive
+            // so that the application can no longer use the device.
+            State prev = State::Alive;
+            if (mState.compare_exchange_strong(prev, State::BeingDisconnected,
+                                               std::memory_order::acq_rel)) {
+                // Ignore errors so that we can continue with destruction
+                // Assume all commands are complete after WaitForIdleForDestruction (because they
+                // were)
+                IgnoreErrors(mQueue->WaitForIdleForDestruction());
+                IgnoreErrors(TickImpl());
+                mQueue->AssumeCommandsComplete();
+                mState = State::Disconnected;
+            }
         }
-
-        // A real device lost happened. Set the state to disconnected as the device cannot be
-        // used. Also tags all commands as completed since the device stopped running.
-        mQueue->AssumeCommandsComplete();
     } else if (!(allowedErrors & type)) {
         // If we receive an error which we did not explicitly allow, assume the backend can't
         // recover and proceed with device destruction. We first wait for all previous commands to
diff --git a/src/dawn/native/Device.h b/src/dawn/native/Device.h
index 0f7cc6f891c40b11aafe611ed2756598f077e5b1..e541ba7b611725ce9f30df1e960fb575560b98ff 100644
--- a/src/dawn/native/Device.h
+++ b/src/dawn/native/Device.h
@@ -343,7 +343,7 @@ class DeviceBase : public ErrorSink,
     //   - Alive: the device is usable and might have work happening on the GPU timeline.
     //   - BeingDisconnected: the device is no longer usable because we are waiting for all
     //     work on the GPU timeline to finish. (this is to make validation prevent the
-    //     application from adding more work during the transition from Available to
+    //     application from adding more work during the transition from Alive to
     //     Disconnected)
     //   - Disconnected: there is no longer work happening on the GPU timeline and the CPU data
     //     structures can be safely destroyed without additional synchronization.
